course: dictionary
lesson: 15154
title: A word from the school yard
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A schoolyard is the outdoor space next to a school, where children go to play at break time. Writers often use it to set a scene, so the words around it help you picture the place. «At break time, the whole class ran out into the schoolyard.» The children ran outside, so the schoolyard must be outdoors.
explain: Look at the words near it for more clues. «Sam and Ella played tag in the schoolyard until the bell rang.» Tag is a running game, and the bell means break is over, so a schoolyard is a place for playing outside.
explain: Some stories use schoolyard in a different way. «The schoolyard bully took Max's lunch every day.» A bully is someone who picks on others, so here schoolyard describes where this happens. The phrase is a clue to the mood of the story.
fact: A schoolyard is the outdoor area next to a school where children play at break time.
fact: The word schoolyard is mostly used in the US and Canada. In the UK, people usually say playground for the same kind of place.
fact: Common phrases include «schoolyard games», «schoolyard bully» and «in the schoolyard».
fact: To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence: «The children played in the playground» still makes sense, so schoolyard and playground mean about the same thing.
exercise: «At break time, the whole class ran out into the schoolyard.» Where did the children go? | The library at the top of the school | *The outdoor area next to the school | Home on the bus | A shop down the road
exercise: «The schoolyard bully took Max's lunch every day.» What does schoolyard bully most likely mean? | A teacher who helps the class | A school bus driver | A pet dog that lives at the school | *A child who picks on other children at school
exercise: Ella in London says, «I'll meet you in the playground.» Her cousin in Ohio says, «I'll meet you in the schoolyard.» Are they talking about the same kind of place? | No, one is a shop | *Yes, both mean the outdoor area next to a school | No, one is inside the school building | No, one is a park far away
test: «Sam and Ella played tag in the schoolyard until the bell rang.» What does the bell tell you? | It is time to go home for the weekend | *Break time is over | Lunch is ready in the hall | A fire has started
test: Which word means nearly the same as schoolyard in a UK story? | Library | Gym hall | *Playground | Classroom
exam: «The schoolyard was loud with laughter and bouncing balls.» What does schoolyard mean here? | A quiet room where children read books | A busy outdoor area next to a school | *A busy outdoor area next to a school at break time, full of children playing | A shop where children buy sweets
